 CompTIA Security+ 701! learning journey

SY0-701) Learning Journey
Overview
This repository documents my learning journey through the CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701) curriculum and serves as a consolidated reference for the security concepts, principles, and practices I studied.
While cybersecurity is often associated with preventing attacks, one of the most important lessons from this course is that security is fundamentally about managing risk, protecting trust, ensuring resilience, and enabling business continuity.
Throughout this learning experience, I explored how organizations defend their systems, data, networks, and users against an evolving threat landscape while maintaining operational reliability.

Key Areas of Study
Identity and Access Management (IAM)
Identity remains the foundation of modern security.
Topics explored include:
-Authentication and authorization
-Access control models
-Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)
-Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)
-Principle of Least Privilege
-Just-In-Time (JIT) access
-Temporary privileged accounts
-Privileged Access Management (PAM)
-Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)
-Federation and Single Sign-On (SSO)

Security Architecture and Network Security
Modern security assumes that breaches may occur and focuses on limiting their impact.
Topics explored include:
-Network segmentation
-Subnetting
-Defense in Depth
-Zero Trust concepts
-Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)
-Air-gapped systems
-Isolation and containment strategies
-Secure network design
-Security zones and trust boundaries

